# `tx.send(v)` fails with core.prelude's `SendError<T>` when the channel
# refuses the value, and `err.value` hands the value back: a send after the
# receiver closed is recovered with `??`, a bare send propagates out of an
# inferred function and converts to the default Err with a fixed message, on
# O0, O1 and O2. That is the law in spec.md's Channels text ("A bare send
# propagates it, and it converts to the default `Err` as `send on a closed
# channel`"): `SendError` is a Core error family member
# (e68a01aeab), and a family member is no typed member of an inferred failure
# union (sema_failure_union_typed, FailureUnion.jet), so `relay` fails with Err
# and has no `value` to hand back. closed.jet sends inside a `Closed`
# contract, which a `SendError<Int>` does not fit: a typed failure with no
# route into the caller's contract is E2404, as
# Tests/UI/E2404_alloc_error_into_never.jet pins for AllocError.
# handled.jet handles that send and checks.
fixture: RefusedSendHandsTheValueBackInSendError.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
env: JET_STORE_DIR={work}/cache
step: jet run -O0 main.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "first\nrefused second\nrelay: send on a closed channel\nforward: send on a closed channel\n"
step: jet run -O1 main.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "first\nrefused second\nrelay: send on a closed channel\nforward: send on a closed channel\n"
step: jet run -O2 main.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "first\nrefused second\nrelay: send on a closed channel\nforward: send on a closed channel\n"
step: jet check closed.jet
exit: 1
stderr: ~ "Jet.ErrorNotPropagatable" # the send's failure has no route into the Closed contract
stderr: ~ "SendError" # the report names the refused send's error
step: jet run -O0 handled.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "5\n5\n"
